WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
In the United States, there are four native venomous snake species.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Coral snakes, rattlesnakes, copperheads, and cottonmouths, also known as water moccasins.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.000
If you or someone is bitten, you should always assume the bite could be venomous.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Before we talk about what to do, we need to clear up some dangerous myths.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.000
If someone is bitten by a snake, do not try to suck out or cut the venom out.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Do not apply ice, and do not use a tight tourniquet.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.000
These old-school remedies actually cause much more tissue damage, and increase the risk of infection.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.000
Instead, ensure the snake is gone, so no one else gets bitten.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Then call 911 immediately.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
While you wait for help, keep the person as still as possible to slow the spread of venom through their body,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
and gently wash the bite with soap and water, and cover it with a clean dressing.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Finally, you'll want to remove any rings, watches, or tight clothing near the bite right away,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
because swelling can happen so fast that these items will cut off circulation.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:09.000
You can use a loose splint or sling to keep the limb at heart level, not elevated high, until professional help arrives.

